# zen-term: workspaces reference
#
# Copy to ~/.config/zen-term/workspaces (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zen-term/workspaces) and
# add a section per workspace. This list is deliberate and hand-curated. zen-term does
# NOT scan any directory. Each section becomes an entry in the ⌘P picker.
#
# Format: INI-style. A `[Title]` line opens a section; `key = value` lines below it
# configure that workspace until the next header. Lines starting with `#` and blank
# lines are ignored; unknown keys are ignored. A section with no `path` is skipped, a
# malformed `env` line is dropped, a `carry` entry that leaves the workspace is dropped,
# and nothing here can crash the app (bad lines are logged and skipped). Manage workspaces
# from inside the app too, in Settings → Workspaces (add / edit / delete / reorder), or the
# ＋ row in the ⌘P picker to add-and-open one.
# Hand-edits to this file are picked up the next time you open the ⌘P picker (or on
# restart).
#
# The section header is the title: it's shown in the picker and pinned as the tab name,
# so it's independent of the folder name (`[ZenTerm]` can point at ~/Dev/zen-term).
#
# Order: the picker lists workspaces in the order their sections appear here, so to put
# one first, move its whole `[Title]` block up. ⌥↑/⌥↓ on a row in Settings → Workspaces
# does the same edit for you. A comment sitting directly on a header moves with it; a
# comment with a blank line under it stays where it is, so a banner at the top of the
# file keeps its place.
#
# Opening a workspace applies its "recipe": the first pane runs `main`, the drawers named
# by `right`/`bottom` open running their commands, focus lands on `focus`, and `env` is
# injected into every pane and drawer. A section with only a `path` is the minimal
# default: one shell pane, both drawers collapsed, cwd = the workspace dir.
#
# Fields:
#   [Title]  (required)  section header → picker label + pinned tab name
#   path     (required)  working directory (`~` is expanded)
#   main     (optional)  first-pane program; absent or `shell` → a plain shell
#   right    (optional)  right-drawer command; absent → closed; `shell` → a plain shell
#   bottom   (optional)  bottom-drawer command; absent → closed; `shell` → a plain shell
#   focus    (optional)  main | right | bottom                       (default main)
#   env      (optional)  KEY=VALUE, repeatable; injected into every surface
#   carry    (optional)  a path to copy into a new worktree, repeatable
#
# A value with spaces may be quoted: bottom = "npm run dev".
#
# About carry: a git worktree gets everything git tracks and nothing it ignores, so a
# fresh one has no .env, no node_modules and no virtualenv, and cannot run the project
# until you install it again. Name here what a worktree of this workspace needs to run.
# On APFS the copy shares its disk blocks with the original, so carrying a node_modules
# costs close to no space and close to no time. On another volume it is a real copy,
# which still works and takes longer. Paths are relative to the workspace and may not
# leave it. A nested path works: config/credentials/development.key is copied there, and
# any directory it needs on the way is created.
#
# What carry will not do: an entry git tracks is refused, because a tracked file copied
# into a worktree makes git report a change that never goes away. An entry the worktree
# already has is left alone. An entry this workspace does not have is skipped, so one
# section can cover a repo before and after its first install.
#
# A carried install is the one from this checkout. Open a worktree on a branch that
# changed a lockfile and the install is still the old one until you run your package
# manager in it.
#
# Setting carry: the workspace form (Settings → Workspaces, or the ＋ row in the ⌘P
# picker) calls this "Copy into new worktrees". It lists what git ignores in the folder
# and you tick what to copy. Editing this file by hand does the same thing.
#
# Making a worktree: ⌥⏎ over a row in the ⌘P picker. Name the branch, pick where it
# starts, and the carry entries below come across with it.
#
# Example: an editor + AI + dev-server workspace (uncomment and edit, or write your own):
#
# [ZenTerm]
# path   = ~/Dev/zen-term
# main   = nvim
# right  = claude
# bottom = shell
# focus  = main
# env    = NODE_ENV=development
# env    = PORT=3000
# carry  = .env
# carry  = node_modules
# carry  = config/credentials/development.key
